WEBVTT

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:20.040
<v Ira Glass>Desperate times, my friends, call for desperate measures. Ora was in college when something mysterious happened. She was at her computer editing.
<v Ira Glass>朋友们，绝望的时刻需要绝望的手段。奥拉上大学时，发生了一件神秘的事情。当时她正在电脑前剪辑。

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:35.240
<v Ora>And I was editing, editing, looking at this computer, and my eyesight started to go blurry. And I just couldn't really see the computer that well. And I was like, I need to stop. I can't really see anymore. And I was walking home, and everything's just blurry.
<v Ora>我当时在剪辑，盯着电脑，视力开始模糊。我真的看不清电脑。我当时想，我得停下来，我真的看不见了。我走回家时，眼前的一切都是模糊的。

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:39.400
<v Ira Glass>An eye doctor examined her, said there was nothing wrong with her eyes to cause the blurriness.
<v Ira Glass>一位眼科医生检查了她，说她的眼睛没有问题，不会导致视力模糊。

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:50.000
<v Ora>And then-- oh, I remember I had weird heart palpitations. So I went to the doctor to get an EKG, and they were like, you seem fine. Maybe you're just in love.
<v Ora>然后——哦，我记得我有过奇怪的心悸。所以我去看医生做了心电图，他们说，你看起来很正常。也许你只是坠入爱河了。

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.880
<v Ira Glass>Pause on that. That was really a thing that happened to you? You showed up with medical symptoms, and they said, maybe you're in love?
<v Ira Glass>暂停一下。这真的是发生在你身上的事吗？你带着病症去看医生，他们说，也许你坠入爱河了？

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:57.000
<v Ora>Yeah.
<v Ora>是的。

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:07.440
<v Ira Glass>She was actually falling in love, but she did not think that explained the heart palpitations. She was starting to get the feeling that people were not taking her seriously. And things were getting worse.
<v Ira Glass>她确实在坠入爱河，但她认为这无法解释心悸。她开始感觉到人们没有认真对待她。情况变得越来越糟。

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:25.640
<v Ora>It just feels like everything in my body is breaking in a weird way-- terrible fatigue, horrible brain fog, and this feeling in the left side of my brain. Just, like, the best way I can describe it is I feel like an ice cream scoop has been taken out of my head.
<v Ora>感觉就像我身体里的每一部分都在以一种奇怪的方式崩坏——极度的疲劳、可怕的脑雾，以及左脑的那种感觉。我能描述的最好的方式是，我觉得我的大脑像是被挖掉了一勺冰淇淋。

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:55.640
<v Ira Glass>Symptoms like that come and go for two years-- blurry vision. Her legs are weak and tremble. Her throat swells up, and it feels like she's being strangled. And then some stuff is constant. She's exhausted, but can't sleep-- pressure headaches. It's hard to think. This derails her life. She's always taking time off from work or showing up late in the day. Can't be on the computer for too long, which limits what jobs she can get. She's going to the doctor all the time. She's too tired to go out with friends at night. Remember, she's in her 20s.
<v Ira Glass>像这样的症状来来去去持续了两年——视力模糊。她的腿无力且颤抖。她的喉咙肿胀，感觉像是被人扼住咽喉。而且有些症状是持续的。她精疲力竭，却无法入睡——压迫性头痛。思考变得很困难。这摧毁了她的生活。她总是请假或迟到。不能在电脑前待太久，这限制了她能从事的工作。她一直在看医生。她太累了，晚上无法和朋友出去玩。记住，她才20多岁。

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:23.920
<v Ira Glass>A tip on Reddit-- of course, Reddit-- leads her to believe that she may have Lyme disease, even though she'd already been tested for Lyme disease, and it came up negative. No Lyme. A different doctor now tests her again, and this time, she tests positive for Lyme. So the doctor prescribes the standard treatment for Lyme, doxycycline, this antibiotic-- doesn't work for her. Then she spends two years on other antibiotics and other drugs. Those fail, too.
<v Ira Glass>Reddit上的一个建议——当然是Reddit——让她相信自己可能患有莱姆病，尽管她已经接受过莱姆病检测，结果是阴性。没有莱姆病。现在另一位医生重新给她做了测试，这次呈阳性。于是医生开了莱姆病的标准治疗药物——多西环素，这种抗生素对她没用。然后她又花了两年时间服用其他抗生素和药物。那些也失败了。

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:46.240
<v Ira Glass>Maybe-- it's possible-- this was because she didn't really have Lyme disease. That second test she took for Lyme was not an FDA-approved test. This is the nightmarish world of Lyme, this whole debate over who really has it and who just has symptoms. From Ora's point of view, the question was moot. She had the symptoms. She wanted them gone.
<v Ira Glass>也许——有可能——这是因为她根本就没有患莱姆病。她做的第二次莱姆病测试并非美国食品药品监督管理局（FDA）批准的测试。这就是莱姆病的噩梦世界，关于谁真正患病而谁只是有症状的争论。从奥拉的角度来看，这个问题毫无意义。她有症状。她只想摆脱它们。

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:56.880
<v Ira Glass>Regular doctors weren't doing it, so she started looking at all the other treatments that people try for these symptoms-- and there are a lot of them. She does sessions in a hyperbaric chamber, gets inside an infrared sauna.
<v Ira Glass>正规医生做不到，所以她开始尝试人们为这些症状尝试的所有其他治疗方法——而且有很多。她进行了高压氧舱疗程，进了红外线桑拿房。

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:20.240
<v Ora>I tried this, like, elimination diet, where all I could eat was, like, grapefruit and goat cheese and oatmeal. I tried, like, a very intense regimen of Chinese herbs that a doctor in New York City invented and manufactured himself. But at every turn, you're like, am I being preyed upon? I'm spending so much money.
<v Ora>我尝试过这种“排除饮食法”，我只能吃葡萄柚、山羊奶酪和燕麦片。我还尝试了一种非常强烈的中国草药疗法，那是一位纽约医生自己发明并制造的。但每走一步，你都会想，我是在被猎食吗？我花了很多钱。

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:41.080
<v Ira Glass>And this drags on for years. She's floating in this unpleasant vortex, an interstellar medical space where nobody's got answers. Things get so bad that she leaves her job in the middle of her contract, because it was too hard to make it through the day. She moves from New York to the Arizona desert, thinking that might help. And now it's five years since her symptoms first appeared.
<v Ira Glass>这拖了好几年。她漂浮在这个令人不适的漩涡中，一个没有人能给出答案的医学星际空间。情况变得非常糟糕，以至于她在合同期中途辞职了，因为很难坚持过每一天。她从纽约搬到了亚利桑那州的沙漠，认为那可能会有帮助。现在离她第一次出现症状已经过去五年了。

00:03:41.080 --> 00:04:06.680
<v Ora>I was just looking down this road that looked really bleak. And this was around the time when I was working with a chronic disease life coach, who was starting to say things like, well, Ora, what would it be like if you never get better? Why don't we start thinking about that, like to have radical acceptance? And I felt very resistant to that, but teetering on the precipice of giving up.
<v Ora>我当时只是看着这条看起来非常黯淡的道路。那大约是在我与一位慢性病生活教练合作的时候，她开始说，嗯，奥拉，如果你永远好不了会怎样？我们为什么不开始思考这个问题，比如去进行“激进的接纳”？我对这感到非常抗拒，但也处于放弃的边缘。

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:23.120
<v Ira Glass>It was at this point that Ora's mom heard about somebody in Ora's situation who supposedly got cured and sent Ora to that doctor. And he suggested a treatment where he was going to put a common product-- this is something you get at any drugstore for cheap-- into her veins with an IV.
<v Ira Glass>就在这时，奥拉的妈妈听说了一位和奥拉处境相似的人据称被治愈了，于是把奥拉送到了那位医生那里。他建议了一种治疗方法，要把一种普通产品——在任何药店都能廉价买到的东西——通过静脉注射输入她的血管。

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:43.080
<v Ira Glass>I am not going to tell you what this product is because this is so unproven. The Centers for Disease Control has said there is no evidence that this works. In fact, this is a toxin. It would do equal damage to healthy and diseased cells. The Infectious Diseases Society of America has warned against trying this because the dangers outweigh these utterly unproven benefits. But this doctor--
<v Ira Glass>我不会告诉你这种产品是什么，因为它太未经证实了。美国疾病控制与预防中心曾表示，没有证据表明它有效。事实上，这是一种毒素。它会对健康细胞和病变细胞造成同样的伤害。美国传染病协会曾警告不要尝试这种方法，因为风险远大于这些完全未经证实的收益。但是这位医生——

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:55.940
<v Ora>He thought that would cure it, but he seems very confident. And his explanation for why I hadn't heard of this before was because it's so accessible and so cheap and so affordable, big pharma doesn't want you to have it.
<v Ora>他认为这能治愈，他显得非常有信心。对于我为什么以前没听说过这个，他的解释是因为它太容易获得、太便宜、太实惠了，大制药公司不想让你拥有它。

00:04:55.940 --> 00:05:06.480
<v Ira Glass>Now, when someone says an argument like that to you, are you the kind of person who's like, yes, I'm in because I hate big pharma, or are you the kind of person who's like, I don't know, that's what crazy people say?
<v Ira Glass>现在，当有人对你提出这种论点时，你是那种会说“我加入，因为我讨厌大制药公司”的人，还是那种会说“我不知道，那是疯子才说的话”的人？

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:13.460
<v Ora>I'm both. It's like, I'm always open, but I'm also very aware of how crazy it sounds.
<v Ora>我两者都是。就像，我总是持开放态度，但我也非常清楚这听起来有多疯狂。

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.840
<v Ira Glass>It did sound crazy to you?
<v Ira Glass>这听起来确实很疯狂吗？

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:23.880
<v Ora>Yeah, I think anytime a medical doctor is telling you something that sounds like a conspiracy theory, it's warrant for concern.
<v Ora>是的，我认为任何时候当一位医学医生告诉你一些听起来像阴谋论的事情时，那都是值得担心的理由。

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.380
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:41.760
<v Ora>But at this point, I'm like-- I mean, I can't think clearly. I'm so tired. I'm so eager to move on with my life that I'm really feeling willing to try anything. And I think I didn't do as much research as one might imagine.
<v Ora>但到了这个地步，我心想——我是说，我无法清晰地思考。我太累了。我太渴望继续我的生活了，以至于我真的觉得愿意尝试任何事。而且我觉得我做的研究不像人们想象的那么多。

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.600
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>I did more research than her.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>我做的研究比她多。

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:56.480
<v Ira Glass>This is Ora's little sister, Aviva, joining the interview. And the way I heard about this story is that Aviva works here at the radio show, Aviva DeKornfeld. She'd watched her sister suffer for years and worried about her. But this treatment?
<v Ira Glass>这是奥拉的妹妹阿维娃，加入到采访中。我听说这个故事是因为阿维娃就在这个广播节目工作，阿维娃·德科恩菲尔德。她看着姐姐受苦多年，为她担忧。但这种治疗？

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:09.440
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>I was very scared. I mean, I was really fixated on this article that I sent her about this woman who had died, who did the IV and then, within 36 hours, was dead. And I thought, doesn't seem worth it.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>我非常害怕。我是说，我当时非常执着于我发给她的那篇文章，关于一个女人做了静脉注射，然后在36小时内就去世了。我想，这看起来不值得。

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:17.440
<v Ira Glass>It's true. The danger of this particular treatment is that it might kill you with, again, no solid studies showing that it works.
<v Ira Glass>确实。这种特殊治疗的危险在于它可能会要了你的命，而且再次重申，没有可靠的研究表明它有效。

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.800
<v Ira Glass>And when you showed that to her, what did she say?
<v Ira Glass>当你把那个拿给她看时，她说什么了？

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:38.200
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>She was freaked out by it, obviously, but was just kind of like, the current state and how I feel also isn't sustainable. My preference would be to have a sister who is sick and alive, rather than a dead sister.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>她显然被吓坏了，但只是觉得，目前的状态以及我的感觉也是不可持续的。我宁愿要一个生病但活着的姐姐，也不要一个死去的姐姐。

00:06:38.200 --> 00:07:09.060
<v Ora>It's like you prefer to have a sister who is sick and alive than one who is dead. And it's-- not to be overly dramatic, I certainly didn't want to die, but I was like, I don't want to be the sister who's sick and alive. This really sucks. And my current state is just so unpleasant and so confused and exhausted, it was just reaching a point of this is not a way to really live. If there was a chance it could work, I wanted to try it.
<v Ora>就像你宁愿要一个生病但活着的姐姐，也不要一个已经去世的。而且这——并不是要过度戏剧化，我当然不想死，但我当时想，我不想成为那个生病但活着的姐姐。这真的很糟糕。我目前的状态是如此不愉快，如此困惑和疲惫，这真的到了一个地步，这不是真正的生活方式。如果有一丝可能奏效，我想试试。

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.000
<v Ira Glass>This is like a Hail Mary pass.
<v Ira Glass>这就像是一个“万福玛丽”传球（孤注一掷）。

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.120
<v Ora>Yeah, total Hail Mary.
<v Ora>是的，完全是孤注一掷。

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:37.520
<v Ira Glass>So, fully knowing that this could kill her, she got the IVs, four of them over a few weeks. And this is not really the result I would have predicted. Her symptoms went away-- the ice cream scoop in her head, the brain fog, the fatigue, the achiness, all of it. Maybe the IV treatment had something to do with this. Maybe it didn't.
<v Ira Glass>所以，在完全知道这可能会要了她的命的情况下，她接受了静脉注射，几周内做了四次。结果真的不是我能预测到的。她的症状消失了——脑子里的冰淇淋勺，脑雾，疲劳，疼痛，所有的这一切。也许静脉注射治疗与此有关。也许无关。

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:53.440
<v Ira Glass>I've now spoken with three Lyme experts and researchers who could not explain this result. They said this is the kind of thing that you would want to do a proper scientific study on because the world is filled with things that seem to work in one isolated case and then really don't prove out.
<v Ira Glass>我采访了三位莱姆病专家和研究人员，他们无法解释这个结果。他们说，这正是那种你想进行正式科学研究的事情，因为世界上充满了在个案中似乎有效，但最终无法证实的东西。

00:07:53.440 --> 00:08:16.920
<v Ira Glass>I also managed to reach Ora's doctor, and the one thing that he said to me in an email was that he was convinced that it was not the IV that ended her symptoms, but one of the other things that he tried with her. He'd given her other supplements and pills. And then he ghosted me without saying what that thing was that he thought worked, wouldn't reply to repeated emails and texts.
<v Ira Glass>我也设法联系到了奥拉的医生，他在一封电子邮件中对我说的唯一一件事是，他坚信结束她症状的不是静脉注射，而是他尝试过的其他东西之一。他给了她其他补充剂和药丸。然后他就不理我了，也没说他认为有效的那个东西是什么，对我的反复邮件和短信不予回复。

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:42.740
<v Ira Glass>And Ora, when she looks back on it now, she thinks it's kind of nuts. She's glad she was cured, for sure, but to be that desperate that you feel like you have no other choice so you throw everything you have into something that not only seems unlikely to succeed sometimes is genuinely terrifying. That is not a place anybody ever wants to be, like this IV treatment.
<v Ira Glass>现在回想起来，奥拉觉得这有点疯狂。她当然很高兴自己被治愈了，但绝望到觉得自己别无选择，以至于把一切都押在一种不仅看起来不太可能成功、有时甚至让人真正感到恐惧的事情上。那不是任何人想待的地方，就像这种静脉注射治疗一样。

00:08:42.740 --> 00:09:09.600
<v Ora>It doesn't seem like a good idea. You can't look back on it and be like, well, given the information we had at the time, we made the best decision we could. It's like, no, that was a bad idea. And so if it turns out bad, then it's like, you have no one to blame but yourself. But if it turns out good-- and I guess that's what the Hail Mary is, is a bad idea that has the potential to work.
<v Ora>这看起来不是个好主意。你不能回过头来看待它时说，好吧，鉴于当时我们掌握的信息，我们做了能做的最好决定。这就像是，不，那是坏主意。所以如果结果不好，那就是你只能怪自己。但如果结果好了——我猜这就是孤注一掷的意义，一个有潜力奏效的坏主意。

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:32.760
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, people in bad situations with no other options going for crazy long shots they hope against hope are going to fix the impossible situations they found themselves in. Our entire episode today, my friends, happens in the fourth quarter, seconds left in the game, after coming from 29 points behind, far, far from the basket, throwing a ball that really looks like it's going to be short. When does that ever work out?
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的节目，关注的是在没有其他选择的糟糕处境下，为了希望能修补那些陷入困境的局面，而去孤注一掷的人们。我的朋友们，我们今天的整期节目都发生在第四节，比赛还剩几秒，在落后29分之后，距离篮筐很远，投出的球看起来真的很像是要投短了。那什么时候会成功呢？

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:51.520
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:09:51.520 --> 00:10:17.680
<v Ira Glass>This American Life, Act 1, 12 Weeks Notice. So some jobs, the entire premise of the job is that every day, you're going to go up against impossible odds, on a mission that is probably not going to succeed. And then, you have to go to work, throw yourself into it, try to make it happen, day after day. There's a new series by our coworkers at Serial about people like that. It's about defense attorneys who work with death row inmates.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》，第一幕，12周通知。有些工作，其整个前提就是你每天都要面对不可能的困难，执行一项很可能不会成功的任务。然后，你必须去上班，全身心投入，尝试让它实现，日复一日。我们的同事在《Serial》做了一个关于这类人的新系列。它是关于那些为死囚辩护的辩护律师。

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:39.320
<v Ira Glass>In Texas, where this particular story takes place, these kinds of appeals to get people off death row fail 94% of the time. And hearing this series, for all the news stories and movies that I've seen about lawyers in that job, it showed sides of it that really surprised me. I don't think I really got some things about the people who do that job and what that job is, until I heard this series.
<v Ira Glass>在得克萨斯州，也就是这个特殊故事发生的地方，这类让人们脱离死囚牢房的上诉有94%的几率会失败。听这个系列节目时，尽管我见过许多关于律师从事这项工作的纪录片和电影，但它展现了一些让我真正感到惊讶的一面。直到听了这个系列，我才真正理解那些从事这项工作的人以及这份工作究竟意味着什么。

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:52.090
<v Ira Glass>And so they allowed us to pull a few scenes from the first two episodes to play for you here so you can get a sense of this. We'll start at the top of the show. Here's how they open the series. Maurice Chammah of the Marshall Project is the host.
<v Ira Glass>因此，他们允许我们从前两集中提取一些片段在这里播放，让大家感受一下。我们将从节目开头开始。这就是他们开篇的方式。马歇尔项目（The Marshall Project）的莫里斯·查马（Maurice Chammah）是主持人。

00:10:52.090 --> 00:11:08.850
<v Maurice Chammah>In the fall of 2024, a criminal defense lawyer got the kind of phone call that most lawyers can only dream about. It concerned a client of his, a guy named David Wood, one of Texas's most notorious serial killers. He was sitting on death row, months away from his execution.
<v Maurice Chammah>2024年秋天，一位刑事辩护律师接到了大多数律师梦寐以求的那种电话。它涉及他的一位客户，一个名叫大卫·伍德的人，他是得克萨斯州最臭名昭著的连环杀手之一。他当时坐在死囚牢房里，离处决还有几个月。

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:20.050
<v George Hall>I would like to discuss with you something about David Wood. While I don't condone what has happened in that case, the fact is I don't like--
<v George Hall>我想和你讨论一些关于大卫·伍德的事情。虽然我不宽恕那个案件中发生的事情，但事实是我不喜欢——

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:48.410
<v Maurice Chammah>The man on the phone, George Hall, thought something corrupt had gone down in David Wood's trial. It had happened more than 30 years ago, but for 30 years, he'd said nothing, mostly because he was afraid to. For those 30 years he'd been on parole, he worried if he aggravated the authorities, they might find some way to send him back to prison. But now his parole had ended, and George Hall was ready to tell his story, which he'd eventually put into a sworn declaration. That story goes like this.
<v Maurice Chammah>打电话的人乔治·霍尔认为大卫·伍德的审判中存在腐败。那是30多年前发生的事，但30年来他什么都没说，主要是因为他害怕。在那30年里他一直处于假释期，他担心如果激怒了当局，他们可能会找到某种方式把他送回监狱。但现在他的假释期结束了，乔治·霍尔准备讲述他的故事，他最终将其写入了一份宣誓声明。那个故事是这样的。

00:11:48.410 --> 00:12:12.570
<v Maurice Chammah>Back in the late '80s, George Hall and David Wood were locked up together at the Eastham Unit in Texas. George Hall was in for murder. David Wood was in for rape. They weren't particularly close. David Wood was quiet, didn't talk much, but when he did, George says, it was mainly to complain about how the police in his hometown of El Paso were harassing him, investigating him for a series of murders.
<v Maurice Chammah>回到80年代末，乔治·霍尔和大卫·伍德一起被关在得克萨斯州的伊斯特姆监狱。乔治·霍尔因谋杀入狱。大卫·伍德因强奸入狱。他们关系并不特别亲近。大卫·伍德很安静，话不多，但乔治说，当他说话时，主要是抱怨他家乡埃尔帕索的警察如何骚扰他，调查他参与的一系列谋杀案。

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:16.930
<v George Hall>Basically, what he said was El Paso was trying to pin it on him.
<v George Hall>基本上，他说的是埃尔帕索警方试图把罪名扣在他头上。

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:38.450
<v Maurice Chammah>David Wood insisted he had nothing to do with those murders. George thought, maybe he didn't, maybe he did. Either way, he didn't really care. Eventually, George and David Wood were separated, moved to different facilities hundreds of miles apart. One day, George says, he's in the prison library, ready to go to lunch, when two officers come in and tell him to pack his stuff.
<v Maurice Chammah>大卫·伍德坚称他与那些谋杀案无关。乔治想，也许他没有，也许他有。不管怎样，他真的不在乎。最终，乔治和大卫·伍德被分开了，搬到了相隔数百英里的不同设施。乔治说，有一天，他正在监狱图书馆准备去吃午饭，两名警官走进来让他收拾东西。

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:45.730
<v George Hall>I said, well, where am I going? They wouldn't tell me. So next thing I know, I'm on a bus ride down to El Paso.
<v George Hall>我说，好吧，我要去哪儿？他们不肯告诉我。所以接下来我知道的是，我坐上一辆巴士去了埃尔帕索。

00:12:45.730 --> 00:13:13.850
<v Maurice Chammah>A few hours later, George finds himself in a holding cell in the El Paso County Jail. Two other guys join him in there. George recognizes them both as guys who had celled with David Wood. And one of them says to the group, they have an opportunity. They can all get money maybe, or less prison time. All they have to do is snitch on David Wood. Soon enough, they're escorted out of the cell and into a car, and George says, that's when the cops start rolling out the red carpet.
<v Maurice Chammah>几个小时后，乔治发现自己被关在埃尔帕索县监狱的一个拘留室里。另外两个人加入了他的行列。乔治认出他们俩都曾和大卫·伍德关在一起。其中一个人对大家说，他们有一个机会。他们都有机会赚钱或者减少刑期。他们所要做的就是举报大卫·伍德。没过多久，他们被带出牢房上了车，乔治说，就在那时，警察开始铺设“红地毯”。

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:28.590
<v George Hall>They give us the tour, a scenic drive up the mountainside, a look across the Rio Grande, look into old Mexico and this and that and whatever. And I'm sitting there, thinking to myself, we're not handcuffed. What if we jump out and run?
<v George Hall>他们带我们参观，开车兜风上山，看一眼格兰德河，看一眼老墨西哥之类的。我坐在那里，心想，我们没戴手铐。如果我们跳下车逃跑怎么办？

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:29.930
<v George Hall>[LAUGHTER]
<v George Hall>[笑声]

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:32.250
<v George Hall>What are they going to do then, you know?
<v George Hall>他们能怎么办呢？

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:45.250
<v Maurice Chammah>But he sits tight. George says they're taken to a hamburger joint for lunch and then, ultimately, to a police station. The guys are offered coffee, snacks, cigarettes, and then they're ushered into a room with detectives.
<v Maurice Chammah>但他还是坐着没动。乔治说他们被带到一家汉堡店吃午饭，最终被带到了警察局。这些人被招待了咖啡、零食、香烟，然后被带进了一个与侦探谈话的房间。

00:13:45.250 --> 00:14:23.570
<v George Hall>Of course, they got files everywhere. They got David Woods's name plastered all over everything. They got arrows and lines going to this, this, dates wrote down. Files are sitting there. They start handing us files. Look at this. We got this on him. We got this on him. He did this. We know this, this, this, this, and going through all facts and stuff there, this narrative-driven shit. And you're reading what they got. And then after that, they go, you know anything? Well, I don't know a goddamn thing. All they had to do is ask me at the prison unit. I was madder than shit about it.
<v George Hall>当然，他们到处都是档案。大卫·伍德的名字被贴满了所有地方。他们有指向这个那个的箭头和线条，写下的日期。档案就在那里。他们开始把档案递给我们。看看这个。我们掌握了他的这个证据。掌握了他的那个。他做了这个。我们知道这个这个这个，过一遍事实之类的，这种充满叙事的垃圾。你读着他们掌握的内容。然后在那之后，他们问，你知道什么吗？好吧，我什么都不知道。他们当初在监狱单位问我就行了。我对此非常生气。

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:25.330
<v Maurice Chammah>As for the other two guys--
<v Maurice Chammah>至于另外两个人——

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:43.170
<v George Hall>They go back. They're talking to each other, but it's in a real low tone. And they basically don't even want to talk to me about nothing. So I knew to myself right then and there, they're going to say whatever they want them to say.
<v George Hall>他们回去了。他们互相交谈，但语气非常低沉。他们基本上甚至不想和我谈论任何事情。所以我当时就知道，他们打算说出警察想让他们说的任何话。

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:55.930
<v Maurice Chammah>They're going to tell the police that David Wood confessed to multiple murders. But George is sure these guys don't actually know anything about David Wood. He would have heard about it already. Plus, he knows they're not above lying.
<v Maurice Chammah>他们将告诉警方大卫·伍德承认犯下了多起谋杀案。但乔治肯定这些人根本不知道关于大卫·伍德的任何事。他早就应该听说过了。此外，他知道他们不是那种不撒谎的人。

00:14:55.930 --> 00:15:13.610
<v Maurice Chammah>George returns to prison. Not long after, he writes to an El Paso prosecutor about, quote, "improprieties that I am aware of." He says he knows the informants are fabricating their stories. The prosecutor never writes back, but the letter does make its way into David Wood's case file.
<v Maurice Chammah>乔治回到监狱。没过多久，他写信给埃尔帕索的一位检察官，引用了“我所知道的不当行为”。他说他知道那些线人是在捏造故事。检察官从未回信，但那封信最终进入了大卫·伍德的案卷。

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:43.690
<v Maurice Chammah>Eventually, David Wood does go on trial for the murders. George is never called to testify, but the two other guys become the star witnesses for the prosecution. The jury convicts David Wood, and he's sentenced to death. Court documents show that after the trial, one of the informants received $13,000 in reward money. The other got his own capital murder charge dropped.
<v Maurice Chammah>最终，大卫·伍德因谋杀案受审。乔治从未被传唤作证，但另外两人成为了控方的核心证人。陪审团判定大卫·伍德有罪，他被判处死刑。法庭文件显示，审判结束后，其中一名线人获得了1.3万美元的奖金。另一人则撤销了他自己的资本谋杀指控。

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:57.890
<v Maurice Chammah>I identified more than a dozen officers, detectives, and supervisors who were involved in David Wood's case. I wanted to ask them about George Hall. Some were dead, one had dementia, one hung up on me, and others never responded.
<v Maurice Chammah>我确认了十几名参与大卫·伍德案件的警官、侦探和主管。我想问他们关于乔治·霍尔的事。有些人死了，一个患有痴呆症，一个挂断了我的电话，其他人从未回应。

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:23.210
<v Maurice Chammah>But the one detective who did speak to me extensively about the case called George's whole story, quote, "preposterous." George and the two others were brought in together and interviewed by the El Paso Police. That's documented in court records. But the detective said he couldn't imagine his colleagues taking prisoners out for a joyride and showing them case materials in order to get them to snitch.
<v Maurice Chammah>但唯一一位和我详细谈论此案的侦探称乔治的整个故事是“荒谬的”。乔治和另外两人确实被带到一起，接受了埃尔帕索警方的采访。这在法庭记录中有记载。但这位侦探说他无法想象他的同事会带囚犯出去兜风，并向他们展示案件材料以让他们举报。

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:38.250
<v Maurice Chammah>For more than 30 years after David Wood's trial, George kept tabs on the case, googled it from time to time. And in 2024, George saw that David Wood was scheduled for execution, and George was finally off parole, so if he was going to speak up, it was now or never.
<v Maurice Chammah>在大卫·伍德受审后的30多年里，乔治一直留意着这个案子，偶尔会在谷歌上搜索它。2024年，乔治看到大卫·伍德被安排执行死刑，而乔治终于结束了假释，所以如果他要说出来，就是现在或永远没机会了。

00:16:38.250 --> 00:17:12.770
<v George Hall>I don't know if it's going to make a difference whether he gets executed or not. That's not the question. That's not what I got to live with. What I have to live with is, can I live with myself, knowing that I know two people fabricated testimony to get a guy executed, and I don't say anything about it?
<v George Hall>我不知道他是否会被处决是否有任何区别。那不是问题。那不是我要与之共处的问题。我要与之共处的是，我知道有人捏造了证词来处决一个人，而我却什么都不说，我能问心无愧吗？

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:34.690
<v Maurice Chammah>Not long after George Hall called David Wood's lawyer, David Wood's lawyer emailed me, asking me to write about the case. I wasn't surprised. I'm a journalist at a nonprofit called the Marshall Project, where we cover the criminal justice system. I'm the death penalty guy on staff, as gloomy as that sounds. But I was surprised by who was asking-- Greg Wiercioch.
<v Maurice Chammah>在乔治·霍尔给大卫·伍德的律师打电话后不久，大卫·伍德的律师给我发了邮件，让我写写这个案子。我并不惊讶。我是在一家名为“马歇尔项目”的非营利机构工作的记者，我们报道刑事司法系统。我是员工中的死刑报道专家，听起来很阴沉。但我对提出请求的人感到惊讶——格雷格·维尔奇奥奇。

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:52.690
<v Maurice Chammah>I know Greg Wiercioch as a big deal in capital defense work. He's been defending people on death row for decades, and he even stopped one execution by winning at the Supreme Court. I'd asked him for an interview years ago for a book I was writing on the death penalty. He said no. He rarely spoke to reporters.
<v Maurice Chammah>我知道格雷格·维尔奇奥奇是死刑辩护领域的大人物。他几十年来一直在为死囚辩护，甚至通过在最高法院胜诉阻止了一次处决。多年前，我为我写的一本关于死刑的书向他请求采访。他说不。他很少对记者说话。

00:17:52.690 --> 00:18:23.810
<v Maurice Chammah>But now here he was in my inbox. His email was polite and panicky. David Wood's execution date was only 17 weeks away. He wanted me to write about the case and all the problems he saw with it. I was pretty skeptical. I did the hard-hitting research of reading the Wikipedia page about David Wood and, woof, six women and girls, one as young as 14, killed and buried in the desert outside of El Paso. David Wood even got one of those spooky serial killer nicknames, the Desert Killer.
<v Maurice Chammah>但现在他出现在我的收件箱里。他的邮件礼貌而恐慌。离大卫·伍德的执行日期只有17周了。他希望我写写这个案子以及他所看到的所有问题。我非常怀疑。我做了艰苦的调查，阅读了大卫·伍德的维基百科页面，哇，六名女性和女孩，其中一名年仅14岁，被杀害并埋在埃尔帕索城外的沙漠里。大卫·伍德甚至还得到了一个那种诡异的连环杀手绰号，沙漠杀手。

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:46.970
<v Maurice Chammah>Greg wrote to me that David Wood was innocent, that he didn't commit any of these murders. And sure, I did find George Hall's story compelling, but even if those informants were lying at the trial, that doesn't mean David Wood didn't do it. Plus, in order to do the story Greg was pitching, I'd have to reinvestigate it from scratch, all six murders in a matter of weeks. That sounded impossible.
<v Maurice Chammah>格雷格写信给我说大卫·伍德是无辜的，他没有犯下这些谋杀案中的任何一起。当然，我确实觉得乔治·霍尔的故事令人信服，但即使那些线人在审判中撒谎，也不意味着大卫·伍德没做过。此外，为了做格雷格推销的故事，我必须从头开始重新调查，在几周内调查所有六起谋杀案。这听起来不可能。

00:18:46.970 --> 00:19:11.970
<v Maurice Chammah>But I was curious about what Greg was up to, his overall project, trying to sow enough doubt at the last minute in order to save his client's life. I'd seen executions get stayed for procedural claims about execution methods or a defendant's mental fitness. But this wasn't just a claim about an unfair trial. Greg was saying David Wood didn't do it at all, and now, somehow, he's supposed to prove that in a few months.
<v Maurice Chammah>但我很好奇格雷格在做什么，他的整个项目，试图在最后一刻播下足够的怀疑，以挽救他客户的生命。我见过死刑因为关于执行方法或被告心理状况的程序性主张而被暂缓。但这不仅仅是一个关于审判不公的主张。格雷格说大卫·伍德根本没做过，而现在，不知何故，他要在几个月内证明这一点。

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:51.570
<v Maurice Chammah>So I told Greg, I'm not going to do the big feature story on David Wood you're imagining. But what if I follow you around, be there with a microphone as you strategize with your team, hunt for witnesses, and try to persuade people of David Wood's innocence with the clock ticking? Greg had a million reasons to say no. I'm still kind of shocked that he said yes.
<v Maurice Chammah>所以我告诉格雷格，我不会写你想象中的那种关于大卫·伍德的大专题报道。但如果我跟踪你，带着麦克风和你一起与你的团队制定策略、寻找证人，并在时间紧迫的情况下试图说服人们相信大卫·伍德的无辜呢？格雷格有一百万个理由拒绝。我到现在还很惊讶他答应了。

00:19:51.570 --> 00:20:03.290
<v Ira Glass>So that's the premise. Maurice is going to follow this defense team. And before we get to some of what he witnesses doing that, we should run through the evidence against David Wood in this murder conviction.
<v Ira Glass>所以这就是前提。莫里斯将跟随这个辩护团队。在我们讨论他所见证的一些行动之前，我们应该过一遍大卫·伍德在这起谋杀定罪中的证据。

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:06.850
<v Ira Glass>Maurice, thank you for talking me through this right now.
<v Ira Glass>莫里斯，谢谢你现在为我梳理这些。

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:08.410
<v Maurice Chammah>Yeah, thanks for having me here.
<v Maurice Chammah>是的，谢谢邀请我。

00:20:08.410 --> 00:20:22.290
<v Ira Glass>Just a quick heads up for listeners that these crimes are pretty awful and might not be right for every listener or for children to listen to. Maurice, let's just run through it. How solid was the evidence that got David Wood the death penalty?
<v Ira Glass>给听众一个简单的提醒，这些罪行非常可怕，可能不适合每一位听众，也不适合儿童收听。莫里斯，我们快速过一遍。大卫·伍德被判处死刑的证据有多扎实？

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:40.570
<v Maurice Chammah>So the person who really walked us through the evidence was a detective who worked on the case. His name is John Guerrero. He was with the El Paso Police Department, and he's retired now. And he invited my Serial producer, Alvin Melathe, and I to his house in El Paso. He introduced himself as Johnny. It's one of the biggest cases of his career, and he seemed to like talking about it.
<v Maurice Chammah>那么，真正带我们梳理证据的人是一位参与此案的侦探。他的名字叫约翰·格雷罗。他曾在埃尔帕索警察局工作，现在退休了。他邀请我和我的《Serial》制片人阿尔文·梅拉特到他埃尔帕索的家里。他自我介绍叫约翰尼。这是他职业生涯中最大的案件之一，他似乎很喜欢谈论它。

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:56.250
<v John Guerrero>You can ask me, is there any doubt in your mind that this is the man that committed this heinous crimes against these little girls? None whatsoever. None. None.
<v John Guerrero>你可以问我，你心中是否有任何怀疑这个人是犯下这些对小女孩的滔天罪行的人吗？完全没有。一点也没有。没有。

00:20:56.250 --> 00:21:13.610
<v Maurice Chammah>So this begins in the summer of 1987, and there are two county employees working in the desert outside of El Paso. And Johnny says they saw a leg sticking out of the sand. The police checked it out, and they found out that it was a woman named Rosa Maria Casio. She was in her 20s.
<v Maurice Chammah>所以这一切始于1987年的夏天，有两名县政府雇员在埃尔帕索城外的沙漠工作。约翰尼说他们看到一只腿从沙子里露出来。警方进行了调查，发现那是一名名叫罗莎·玛丽亚·卡西奥的女性。她20多岁。

00:21:13.610 --> 00:21:33.250
<v Maurice Chammah>And then they searched the area around her, and pretty quickly, they find a second body about 50 feet away. It's a woman named Karen Baker. She's 20, and she's a mother of three young children. And then over the next few months, they find a total of six bodies in this area of the desert. Two of the victims are girls in middle school, and then their ages run up into their early 20s.
<v Maurice Chammah>然后他们搜查了她周围的区域，很快就在大约50英尺外发现了第二具尸体。那是一名名叫凯伦·贝克的女性。她20岁，是三个年幼孩子的母亲。然后在接下来的几个月里，他们在沙漠的这个区域总共发现了六具尸体。受害者中有两名是初中女生，其余的年龄上升到20岁出头。

00:21:33.250 --> 00:21:34.610
<v Ira Glass>Ugh, middle school kids.
<v Ira Glass>噢，初中的孩子。

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:47.890
<v Maurice Chammah>I know. It's really-- it's awful. The detail that really stuck out to me was that they could identify the girl who'd been in eighth grade in part because they found her T-shirt where all the other kids had signed their names on the last day of school. And--
<v Maurice Chammah>我知道。这真的很——这太糟糕了。那个让我印象深刻的细节是，他们之所以能确认那个八年级女孩的身份，部分原因是因为他们找到了她的T恤，上面签满了所有其他孩子在毕业那天留下的名字。而且——

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:48.330
<v Ira Glass>Ugh.
<v Ira Glass>噢。

00:21:48.330 --> 00:22:01.510
<v Maurice Chammah>--it turns out that that girl's mother becomes a real leading force in this case, and she pushes Johnny to see all of these murders as connected, first of all, and then to go talk to other middle school kids to try to get clues or tips from them about who might have done it.
<v Maurice Chammah>——事实证明，那个女孩的母亲成为了此案真正的领导力量，她首先推动约翰尼将所有这些谋杀案联系起来，然后去和其他中学生交谈，试图从他们那里得到线索或提示，了解可能是谁做的。

00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:06.370
<v Ira Glass>And in fact, they do get clues. Let's play a little bit from the podcast of where you get into that.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，他们确实得到了线索。让我们播放一小段你在播客中深入探讨的内容。

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:18.210
<v John Guerrero>We started getting information from several people about this white guy that was going around in a beige truck and also in a motorcycle.
<v John Guerrero>我们开始从几个人那里得到关于这个开着米色卡车和摩托车的白人男子的信息。

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:25.970
