<v Maurice Chammah>Greg and I finish up at the courthouse around lunchtime. Before we head back to the hotel, he proposes we swing by someone's house first-- a woman he's been trying to talk to for some time now, Ramona Dismukes. Her importance as a witness is kind of questionable. She was best friends with a girl who disappeared back in 1987. A lot of people in El Paso assumed David Wood was responsible, but police never found evidence, and he wasn't convicted of it. It's the kind of witness I could imagine skipping, but Greg decides it's worth a try.
<v Maurice Chammah>格雷格和我在午餐时间左右完成了法院的工作。在我们回到酒店之前，他建议我们先顺道去某人家里——一个他已经尝试联系一段时间的女人，拉莫娜·迪斯穆克斯。她作为证人的重要性有点令人怀疑。她是1987年失踪的一名女孩的最好朋友。埃尔帕索的许多人都认为大卫·伍德负责，但警方从未找到证据，他也没有被定罪。这是一种我可能会跳过的证人，但格雷格决定尝试一下。

00:31:47.410 --> 00:32:02.530
<v Maurice Chammah>We pull off the highway and drive up to this small house where a guy with a mohawk is hauling stuff out to a dumpster. Greg hops out of the car and talks to him. He comes back, looking defeated. But as we're pulling away, I see a woman in the rearview mirror.
<v Maurice Chammah>我们驶离高速公路，开车来到这个小房子，一个留着莫霍克发型的家伙正在把东西搬进垃圾箱。格雷格跳下车和他交谈。他回来时显得很沮丧。但当我们开走时，我在后视镜里看到一个女人。

00:32:02.530 --> 00:32:08.930
<v Greg Wiercioch>Yeah, let me see. Hi. How are you?
<v Greg Wiercioch>是的，让我看看。嗨。你好吗？

00:32:08.930 --> 00:32:12.200
<v Maurice Chammah>Greg leads the woman towards the car, and I roll down my window.
<v Maurice Chammah>格雷格领着那个女人走向车，我摇下窗户。

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:13.080
<v Maurice Chammah>What's your name? I'm sorry.
<v Maurice Chammah>你叫什么名字？抱歉。

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:13.300
<v Greg Wiercioch>Oh, yeah, this is--
<v Greg Wiercioch>哦，是的，这是——

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:14.460
<v Ramona Dismukes>I'm Ramona Dismukes.
<v Ramona Dismukes>我是拉莫娜·迪斯穆克斯。

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:29.440
<v Maurice Chammah>Everyone is half-interrupting each other. Ramona tells us the mohawk guy is actually her husband. She moved out of state and is just back to do renovations. She seems excited to talk to Greg, but says the house is too much of a mess to invite him in. She suggests another spot.
<v Maurice Chammah>每个人都在半打断对方。拉莫娜告诉我们那个莫霍克发型的家伙实际上是她的丈夫。她搬到了外州，只是回来做翻新。她似乎很兴奋和格雷格交谈，但说房子太乱了，不能邀请他进去。她建议去另一个地方。

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:32.220
<v Ramona Dismukes>I mean, there's a Whataburger up the street, right around the corner.
<v Ramona Dismukes>我的意思是，街上有个Whataburger，就在拐角处，对吧？

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:32.340
<v Greg Wiercioch>OK.
<v Greg Wiercioch>好的。

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:32.720
<v Ramona Dismukes>And I can go there--
<v Ramona Dismukes>我可以去那里——

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:36.240
<v Greg Wiercioch>Your husband said you might have files and records still.
<v Greg Wiercioch>你丈夫说你可能还有文件和记录。

00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:43.100
<v Ramona Dismukes>I still have all of the old articles. I have everything.
<v Ramona Dismukes>我还有所有的旧文章。我什么都有。

00:32:43.100 --> 00:32:43.660
<v Greg Wiercioch>All right, to the left.
<v Greg Wiercioch>好的，向左转。

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:45.860
<v Ramona Dismukes>See you in a bit.
<v Ramona Dismukes>一会儿见。

00:32:45.860 --> 00:33:11.900
<v Maurice Chammah>Naomi and Alvin meet us at the Whataburger. As the only native Texan in this group, I feel obligated to say, Whataburger is a state institution, but it is not a great place to do an interview. There's loud music, soda fountains and fryers going, people coming in and out. Ramona blazes in, big, expressive face. Her hair is wild and curly, some of the layers dyed cherry red.
<v Maurice Chammah>娜奥米和阿尔文在Whataburger和我们汇合。作为这群人中唯一的得克萨斯本地人，我感到有义务说，Whataburger是州立机构，但它不是进行采访的好地方。那里有嘈杂的音乐、苏打水机和油炸锅，人们进进出出。拉莫娜风风火火地冲进来，表情丰富。她的头发狂野卷曲，其中几层染成了樱桃红。

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:34.100
<v Maurice Chammah>She slaps down this giant binder, very dusty, that says, "Mona's Articles"-- she also goes by Mona-- in cursive letters next to what looks like a cigarette burn. It's full of old newspaper clippings that leave flakes on the table. Apparently, all those years Greg was developing his theories of what really happened in 1987, Ramona was nursing her own.
<v Maurice Chammah>她重重地拍下这个巨大的、非常多尘的活页夹，上面用草书写着“莫娜的文章”——她也叫莫娜——旁边看起来像是个烟头烫痕。里面全是旧报纸剪报，在桌子上留下了碎片。显然，在格雷格发展他关于1987年到底发生了什么理论的所有那些年里，拉莫娜也在酝酿她自己的理论。

00:33:34.100 --> 00:34:01.400
<v Maurice Chammah>El Paso is a pretty big city, but Ramona talked about it like a small town. David Wood, the police, the victims, their parents-- everybody knows everybody, as if somehow they all went to high school together. Even this Whataburger is relevant, in a way. Ramona tells us she got involved in this case because of her best friend, Cheryl Vasquez. When they were still teenagers, Cheryl married Ramona's brother, which made them best friends and sisters-in-law. And Cheryl--
<v Maurice Chammah>埃尔帕索是个相当大的城市，但拉莫娜谈论起它来就像个小镇。大卫·伍德、警察、受害者、他们的父母——每个人都认识每个人，好像他们都一起上过高中一样。甚至这个Whataburger在某种程度上也是相关的。拉莫娜告诉我们，她卷入这个案子是因为她最好的朋友谢丽尔·瓦斯奎兹。当她们还是青少年时，谢丽尔嫁给了拉莫娜的兄弟，这使她们成为最好的朋友和嫂子。而谢丽尔——

00:34:01.400 --> 00:34:03.920
<v Ramona Dismukes>She worked right here at this Whataburger. This is where she worked.
<v Ramona Dismukes>她就在这个Whataburger工作。这就是她工作的地方。

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:04.820
<v Greg Wiercioch>Wow.
<v Greg Wiercioch>哇。

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:14.420
<v Ramona Dismukes>When she went missing, she was working here. And she never even got her last check from this place because she was gone. But--
<v Ramona Dismukes>当她失踪时，她就在这里工作。她甚至没拿到在这个地方的最后一张支票，因为她不见了。但是——

00:34:14.420 --> 00:34:40.660
<v Maurice Chammah>Cheryl was 19 when she disappeared, the same summer as all the Desert Killer victims. When bodies started turning up in the desert, Ramona was worried that Cheryl was going to be one of them. She felt the cops weren't looking hard enough for her friend, so she enlisted her mom, and they decided to figure out what happened to Cheryl themselves. They knew Cheryl was last seen at this one Circle K convenience store. And so they came up with, frankly, a totally bonkers plan.
<v Maurice Chammah>谢丽尔失踪时19岁，和“沙漠杀手”的所有受害者是同一个夏天。当沙漠里开始出现尸体时，拉莫娜很担心谢丽尔会是其中之一。她觉得警察没有为她的朋友做足够的寻找，所以她拉上了她的妈妈，她们决定自己弄清楚谢丽尔发生了什么。她们知道谢丽尔最后一次出现在这家Circle K便利店。于是她们想出了一个坦率地说完全疯狂的计划。

00:34:40.660 --> 00:34:52.680
<v Ramona Dismukes>We started hanging out at that Circle K. And my mom was literally tricking me. She was like, go sit on that wall, and somebody's going to come and try to kidnap you. And I'll call the cops, and we'll get them and we'll know who it was. And I'm like, OK.
<v Ramona Dismukes>我们开始在那个Circle K徘徊。我妈简直是在骗我。她说，去坐在那堵墙上，会有人来试图绑架你。我会报警，我们会抓住他们，我们就会知道那是谁了。我说，好的。

00:34:52.680 --> 00:34:54.500
<v Greg Wiercioch>Your mom like a real character from what I've--
<v Greg Wiercioch>你妈真是个有个性的人，从我所听到的来看——

00:34:54.500 --> 00:35:05.020
<v Ramona Dismukes>Oh! She was feisty until the moment she died. She had me sit on that wall in little shorts, 17 years old, waiting to get me kidnapped. I'm like, thanks, Mom. But--
<v Ramona Dismukes>噢！她一直是个性十足，直到她去世的那一刻。她让我坐在那堵墙上穿着短裤，17岁，等着被绑架。我说，谢谢你，妈妈。但是——

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:27.900
<v Maurice Chammah>By way of explanation, Ramona says that her mom was, quote, "German." The lawyers don't ask her to explain more. But as strange as the plan was, there was some logic to it. Ramona and Cheryl were about the same age and, according to Ramona, looked so alike that they got mistaken for sisters. Ramona's mom thought she could use her daughter's looks to lure out the Desert Killer.
<v Maurice Chammah>作为解释，拉莫娜说她的妈妈是“德国人”。律师没让她进一步解释。但尽管这个计划很奇怪，它还是有一些逻辑的。拉莫娜和谢丽尔年纪相仿，据拉莫娜说，长得太像了，以至于被误认为是姐妹。拉莫娜的妈妈认为她可以利用女儿的长相引诱出“沙漠杀手”。

00:35:27.900 --> 00:35:44.100
<v Maurice Chammah>Ramona says that she and her mom did the Circle K routine over a few days with no luck. Then, one afternoon, she was sitting on a wall outside the store in her shorts when she saw a truck approach. Word around town was that the Desert Killer drove a beige or a brown truck.
<v Maurice Chammah>拉莫娜说她和妈妈在那家Circle K做了几天的日常任务，没有运气。然后，一天下午，她穿着短裤坐在商店外的墙上时，看到一辆卡车靠近。城里的传言是“沙漠杀手”开着一辆米色或棕色的卡车。

00:35:44.100 --> 00:36:01.900
<v Ramona Dismukes>One day, this brown truck came rolling in and went to the store and came out and looked over at me and was like, oh, shit. Threw a quarter at me-- go home. Huh? Go home. Your family's looking for you. They said I stole you, and the cops have been asking me questions. You need to take your ass home. And I'm like, who are you talking to? And I didn't know--
<v Ramona Dismukes>有一天，这辆棕色卡车开了进来，去了商店，出来后看着我，心想，噢，该死。扔了一枚硬币给我——回家。嗯？回家。你的家人在找你。他们说我偷了你，警察一直在问我问题。你需要带着你的屁股回家。我说，你在跟谁说话？我不知道——

00:36:01.900 --> 00:36:27.540
<v Maurice Chammah>This was how Ramona first met David Wood. At this point, the cops were starting to zero in on him for the disappearances, including Cheryl's, and he knew it. He mistakenly thought Ramona was Cheryl because, again, they looked alike. So that part of the plan actually worked. Ramona says he tossed a quarter at her as in, you're the girl the cops think I abducted. Please use this quarter to call home on the payphone. Tell everyone you're safe.
<v Maurice Chammah>这就是拉莫娜第一次见到大卫·伍德的方式。此时，警察开始集中调查他的失踪案，包括谢丽尔的，而他知道这一点。他错误地认为拉莫娜就是谢丽尔，因为她们长得确实像。所以计划的那部分确实成功了。拉莫娜说他扔了一枚硬币给她，意思是，你是警察认为我绑架的那个女孩。请用这枚硬币在公用电话打回家。告诉大家你是安全的。

00:36:27.540 --> 00:36:46.100
<v Maurice Chammah>Ramona goes on to tell us about the surprising relationship that developed between her, her mom, and David Wood, something between a friendship and a covert op. David Wood himself remembers spending time with the two of them, but what he didn't know is that they were also spying on him. Ramona says her mom started inviting David Wood to their house.
<v Maurice Chammah>拉莫娜继续告诉我们，她、她妈妈和大卫·伍德之间发展出的那种令人惊讶的关系，介于友谊和秘密行动之间。大卫·伍德本人记得和她们俩待在一起的时间，但他不知道的是她们也在监视他。拉莫娜说她妈妈开始邀请大卫·伍德到家里。

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:57.280
<v Ramona Dismukes>He'd come over, she'd give him coffee, they'd hang out, and they'd talk. My mom thought she was slick, and she was. My mom was like, I'm going to sit here and talk to him, you go search his truck.
<v Ramona Dismukes>他过来，她给他咖啡，他们待在一起，他们聊天。我妈妈以为她很滑头，确实也是。我妈妈说，我要坐在这里和他聊天，你去搜他的卡车。

00:36:57.280 --> 00:37:24.440
<v Maurice Chammah>She says she stole David Wood's keys and rifled through his truck, trying to find something incriminating. But she never did. She also saw him get nauseous around blood. She cut her hand in front of him once, which, while not exactly exonerating, didn't scream serial killer either. For Ramona and her mom, it all added up to one thing-- David Wood could not be the Desert Killer. Ramona says her mom was eager to help clear his name.
<v Maurice Chammah>她说她偷了大卫·伍德的钥匙，在卡车里翻来覆去，试图找到定罪的东西。但她从来没找到。她还看到他在血面前会恶心。她有一次在他面前划伤了手，这虽然不能完全洗清罪名，但也并不尖叫着“连环杀手”。对拉莫娜和她妈妈来说，这一切加起来只有一个结论——大卫·伍德不可能是“沙漠杀手”。拉莫娜说她妈妈非常渴望帮忙洗清他的名字。

00:37:24.440 --> 00:37:39.980
<v Ramona Dismukes>My mom immediately called the detectives. He's the wrong guy. You got the wrong guy because he didn't kidnap my daughter, and she looks just like Cheryl. And he threw a quarter at her and told her to go home. And my mom's German-- very feisty, very loud. She's like, da-da-da-da-da. So they're like, oh, we want to come and talk to you.
<v Ramona Dismukes>我妈立刻给侦探打了电话。他是错误的人。你们抓错人了，因为他没绑架我女儿，而她长得和谢丽尔一模一样。他扔了一枚硬币给她叫她回家。我妈是德国人——非常火爆，非常大声。她说，哒哒哒哒哒。所以他们说，噢，我们想过来和你谈谈。

00:37:39.980 --> 00:37:46.320
<v Maurice Chammah>The police came to the house and asked Ramona to come down to the station to give a statement to detectives. She was sketched out.
<v Maurice Chammah>警察来到家里，要求拉莫娜去警察局给侦探一份声明。她感到很烦。

00:37:46.320 --> 00:38:08.460
<v Ramona Dismukes>I said, no, I'm not going to give you a statement. And they said, well, we want to show you something. So they put me in the car and took me out to the desert. And they're like, there's bodies out here, and it'd be really easy for you to be out here. And they wanted me to write a statement. They told me, out there in the desert, we need you to go back and write a statement. We need you to tell us that David Wood tried to take you.
<v Ramona Dismukes>我说，不，我不会给你一份声明。他们说，嗯，我们想给你看点东西。于是他们把我塞进车里，带我去了沙漠。他们说，这里有尸体，你在这里很容易出事。他们要我写一份声明。他们告诉我，在那沙漠里，我们需要你回去写一份声明。我们需要你告诉我们大卫·伍德试图绑架你。

00:38:08.460 --> 00:38:27.900
<v Ramona Dismukes>I said, but he didn't try to take me. He threw a quarter at me and told me to go home. He said, well, that's not what we need you to say. [CHUCKLES] I said, but that's not the truth. They said, you look just like all the other girls that are gone. This could be you. And I was like, are you threatening me? And they're like, well, no, we're just telling you that if you don't put him away, he might take you.
<v Ramona Dismukes>我说，但他没试图绑架我。他扔了一枚硬币给我，叫我回家。他说，嗯，那不是我们需要你说的。[笑声] 我说，但那不是事实。他们说，你长得和所有那些失踪的女孩一模一样。这可能是你。我心想，你们在威胁我吗？他们说，嗯，不，我们只是告诉你，如果你不把他关起来，他可能会绑架你。

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:57.740
<v Maurice Chammah>Ramona says the detectives who drove her out to the desert were Johnny Guerrero and his partner, Alfonso Marquez. She eventually put all of this in a sworn statement to the court. Ramona is not the first person to accuse these guys of abusing their power. There's George Hall, the guy who called Greg with the whole story of the jailhouse informants and the red carpet treatment and later laid out his claims in a sworn declaration. According to trial transcripts, at least one other witness claimed the police tried to add falsehoods to her statement.
<v Maurice Chammah>拉莫娜说带她去沙漠的侦探是约翰尼·格雷罗和他的搭档阿方索·马尔克斯。她最终把所有这些都写进了法庭宣誓声明中。拉莫娜不是第一个指责这些人滥用权力的人。还有乔治·霍尔，那个给格雷格打电话讲述狱中线人、红地毯招待等整个故事，后来在宣誓声明中列出他指控的人。根据审判记录，至少还有另一名证人声称警察试图在她的陈述中增加虚假内容。

00:38:57.740 --> 00:39:30.100
<v Maurice Chammah>In a different case from around this time, a suspect told a reporter that Detective Guerrero bullied him into a false confession. We asked Detective Guerrero about that interrogation, and he said he didn't remember it. He also denied ever taking Ramona out to the desert. Actually, what he said, was, quote, "She's full of shit." Moreover, he told us, in his 15 years in homicide, quote, "I was never accused of any wrongdoing or coercion by anyone I talked to or any case I worked," unquote.
<v Maurice Chammah>在那个时期左右的另一个案件中，一名嫌疑人告诉记者，格雷罗侦探欺凌他以获得虚假供词。我们问了格雷罗侦探关于那次审讯的问题，他说他不记得了。他还否认曾经带拉莫娜去过沙漠。实际上，他说的是“她在胡说八道”。此外，他告诉我们，在他做凶杀案调查的15年里，“我从未被任何我交谈过的人或我处理的任何案件指控有任何不当行为或强迫行为。”

00:39:30.100 --> 00:39:50.100
<v Maurice Chammah>Detective Marquez, for his part, died a couple of years ago. But it is worth noting that he had a reputation for lying and using force. In a different case, he allegedly bullied a 16-year-old kid to make him confess to two murders. The kid was later exonerated after serving nearly 20 years in prison.
<v Maurice Chammah>马尔克斯侦探几年前去世了。但值得注意的是，他有撒谎和使用暴力的名声。在另一个案件中，他据称欺凌了一个16岁的孩子，迫使他承认犯下两起谋杀案。那个孩子在服刑近20年后被平反。

00:39:50.100 --> 00:40:24.820
<v Maurice Chammah>Watching the lawyers interview Ramona, watching them jot down notes at every twist and turn, I was struck by how absurd it was that this was where we found ourselves, 50 days out from the execution, all of us huddled around a table in this Whataburger, the lawyers trying to piece together something useful from this zany story. Ramona is entertaining, but since I'm not steeped in the case, it also seems like she mixes theories and stories and gossip and evidence so effortlessly and at such a rapid clip, that it's a little hard to keep up, much less to accept it all at face value.
<v Maurice Chammah>看着律师采访拉莫娜，看着他们在每一个转折处记下笔记，我感到非常荒谬，这就是我们发现自己所在的地方，距离处决还有50天，我们所有人都挤在Whataburger的一张桌子旁，律师们试图从这个滑稽的故事中拼凑出有用的东西。拉莫娜很有娱乐性，但由于我不了解此案，她似乎毫不费力地以如此快的速度混合理论、故事、八卦和证据，以至于很难跟上，更不用说全盘接受了。

00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:25.600
<v Greg Wiercioch>Yeah, right, right.
<v Greg Wiercioch>是的，对，对。

00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:49.980
<v Maurice Chammah>I look over at Greg and Naomi to see how they're reacting, to get some clue about how seriously I should take all of this, but they're more or less statues. Very stoic, these two lawyers in a Whataburger. All of which to say, I'm not sure how to assess some of the other claims Ramona makes. The most explosive ones concern a very important person in the case against David Wood, Judith Kelling.
<v Maurice Chammah>我看向格雷格和娜奥米看他们的反应，以得到关于我应该多认真对待这一切的线索，但他们或多或少是雕像。在Whataburger的这两位律师非常冷漠。所有这些都是说，我不确定如何评估拉莫娜提出的其他一些主张。最劲爆的主张涉及大卫·伍德案中的一个非常重要的人，朱迪思·凯林。

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:54.780
<v Ramona Dismukes>And when I heard her name, I'm like, this is that prostitute that's saying David raped her.
<v Ramona Dismukes>当我听到她的名字时，我心想，这就是那个说大卫强奸了她的妓女。

00:40:54.780 --> 00:40:56.700
<v Greg Wiercioch>Yes.
<v Greg Wiercioch>是的。

00:40:56.700 --> 00:41:07.620
<v Maurice Chammah>Judith Kelling was the woman who claimed David Wood raped her out in the desert. Ramona says she was out one day, looking for her sister-in-law, who was still missing, when she spotted Kelling and sidled up to her.
<v Maurice Chammah>朱迪思·凯林就是那个声称大卫·伍德在沙漠里强奸她的女人。拉莫娜说有一天她出去寻找仍然失踪的嫂子时，发现了凯林并悄悄接近了她。

00:41:07.620 --> 00:41:20.860
<v Ramona Dismukes>So I'm playing it cool with her, and I'm like, oh, yeah, I'm looking for my sister, and da-da-da-da. And she's like, oh, the David Wood thing. She's like, yeah, that's all bullshit. I was like, what do you mean? And she's like, he didn't do anything to me.
<v Ramona Dismukes>所以我对她表现得冷静，我说，噢，是的，我在找我姐姐，哒哒哒。她说，噢，大卫·伍德那个案子。她说，是的，那全是胡扯。我说，什么意思？她说，他没对我做任何事。

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:22.360
<v Greg Wiercioch>Really?
<v Greg Wiercioch>真的？

00:41:22.360 --> 00:41:22.660
<v Ramona Dismukes>Really.
<v Ramona Dismukes>真的。

00:41:22.660 --> 00:41:28.600
<v Greg Wiercioch>She told you this after David was arrested in October of 1987.
<v Greg Wiercioch>她在大卫1987年10月被捕后告诉你的。

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:32.060
<v Ramona Dismukes>Here after he was arrested, after he was already charged with her rape, what he was charged with before he was--
<v Ramona Dismukes>在这里他被捕后，在他被控强奸她之后，在他——之前——

00:41:32.060 --> 00:41:52.180
<v Maurice Chammah>You can tell in Greg's voice how much he would love to poke holes in Judith Kelling's credibility. It's something he's been trying to do for years. For example, he found one source who gave a sworn statement to the court that Judith Kelling had been a police informant, and specifically that she'd been Detective Johnny Guerrero's informant when she reported her rape.
<v Maurice Chammah>你可以从格雷格的声音中听出他有多想戳穿朱迪思·凯林的可信度。这是他多年来一直试图做的事情。例如，他找到了一个向法庭提供宣誓声明的来源，称朱迪思·凯林曾是警方线人，特别是在她举报强奸时，她是约翰尼·格雷罗侦探的线人。

00:41:52.180 --> 00:42:18.580
<v Maurice Chammah>Judith also spent long stretches in jail around this time on drug charges. On top of that, Greg has noticed that her story of what exactly happened in the desert kept changing, getting more and more aligned with other things the police believed about David Wood and the murders. Greg uses these points to make an argument in court filings. Desperate to solve the murders, Detective Guerrero squeezed an equally desperate Judith Kelling to point the finger at David Wood.
<v Maurice Chammah>朱迪思当时也因为毒品指控在监狱里呆了很长时间。最重要的是，格雷格注意到她在沙漠中究竟发生了什么的故事一直在变，变得越来越符合警察关于大卫·伍德和谋杀案的其他看法。格雷格在法庭文件中利用这些观点进行辩论。急于解决谋杀案，格雷罗侦探挤压同样绝望的朱迪思·凯林，让她指向大卫·伍德。

00:42:18.580 --> 00:42:34.060
<v Maurice Chammah>We asked Detective Guerrero about all of this, and he denies that Kelling was ever his informant. Judith Kelling died a decade ago, so Greg can't ask her about it. But here, in this Whataburger, Ramona claims Kelling revealed much more to her about what really went down.
<v Maurice Chammah>我们问了格雷罗侦探所有这些问题，他否认凯林曾经是他的线人。朱迪思·凯林十年前去世了，所以格雷格无法问她。但在这里，在这个Whataburger，拉莫娜声称凯林向她透露了更多关于真正发生了什么的事情。

00:42:34.060 --> 00:42:45.980
<v Ramona Dismukes>This is what she's telling me. They made a deal with me to get me out of jail, and all I got to do is testify against this guy. I was like, well, were you even raped? She's like, yeah, but not by him. I'm like, who? She said, Mike Plyler. I'm like, who's this guy?
<v Ramona Dismukes>这就是她告诉我的。他们和我做了交易把我弄出监狱，我所要做的就是在这个人身上作证。我说，那你真的被强奸了吗？她说，是的，但不是他。我说，谁？她说，迈克·普莱勒。我说，这个家伙是谁？

00:42:45.980 --> 00:43:02.020
<v Maurice Chammah>Mike Plyler? What? The same guy Greg asked about at the courthouse a few hours ago? The one I thought was a long shot? I look over at Greg, and I can see he's just as surprised. He's barely able to contain himself, nodding so hard it's like he's swaying.
<v Maurice Chammah>迈克·普莱勒？什么？格雷格几个小时前在法院询问的同一个人？我以为那是极不可能的一条线索？我看向格雷格，我能看出他和我一样惊讶，他几乎无法控制自己，点着头，点得非常用力，就像在摇晃一样。

00:43:02.020 --> 00:43:33.300
<v Maurice Chammah>Ramona says that after she got Mike Plyler's name from Judith, she and her mom looked for the guy and, well, basically stalked him. They staked out his apartment, watched him come and go. They were blown away by the similarities between this man and David Wood-- similar builds, similar tattoos. On top of the similar trucks, they also both drove red motorcycles. I can practically see the gears turning in Greg's mind. And as the Whataburger fills with the sound of someone making the world's loudest milkshake, he tosses out one more question.
<v Maurice Chammah>拉莫娜说在从朱迪思那里得到迈克·普莱勒的名字后，她和妈妈找了那个家伙，好吧，基本上跟踪了他。她们盯着他的公寓，看着他来来去去。这个人和大卫·伍德之间的相似之处让她们惊呆了——相似的身材、相似的纹身。除了相似的卡车，他们都开红色的摩托车。我几乎可以看到格雷格脑子里的齿轮在转动。当Whataburger里充满了某人制作世界最大奶昔的声音时，他抛出了最后一个问题。

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:40.740
<v Greg Wiercioch>Do you have any information about this nickname Skeeter that supposedly is--
<v Greg Wiercioch>关于这个叫斯基特的绰号，你有什么信息吗，据说是——

00:43:40.740 --> 00:43:42.820
<v Ramona Dismukes>That's Skeeter. Mike Plyler's Skeeter.
<v Ramona Dismukes>那就是斯基特。迈克·普莱勒就是斯基特。

00:43:42.820 --> 00:43:45.000
<v Greg Wiercioch>Well, when you say that, how do you know that he's Skeeter?
<v Greg Wiercioch>嗯，当你这么说时，你怎么知道他是斯基特？

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:46.580
<v Ramona Dismukes>Because that's what Judith called him.
<v Ramona Dismukes>因为朱迪思就是那么叫他的。

00:43:46.580 --> 00:43:47.120
<v Greg Wiercioch>Is that right?
<v Greg Wiercioch>是吗？

00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:54.500
<v Ramona Dismukes>That's what she called him. She called him Skeeter. So that's Skeeter.
<v Ramona Dismukes>她就是那么叫他的。她叫他斯基特。所以那就是斯基特。

00:43:54.500 --> 00:44:19.140
<v Maurice Chammah>Ramona says that she went to the cops back in 1987 and told them everything she learned, about David Wood, about Judith Kelling, and about Michael Plyler. And, she says, the cops did nothing. They could have, for example, looked more into Plyler, gotten some DNA samples, polygraphed him. Instead, Ramona says, they simply told her to stay away.
<v Maurice Chammah>拉莫娜说她在1987年去了警察局，告诉了他们关于大卫·伍德、朱迪思·凯林和迈克·普莱勒的所有事情。她说，警察什么都没做。例如，他们本可以更多地调查普莱勒，获取一些DNA样本，给他做测谎。拉莫娜说，相反，他们只是告诉她远离。

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:57.020
<v Maurice Chammah>Ramona's whole story is wild, obviously. And as we wrap up at the Whataburger, it's not clear to me how Greg is going to use it all, whether he's going to try to corroborate any of these details. And I'm not sure that's really possible. I can imagine what a prosecutor might say. For example, the Judith Kelling story, that it wasn't David Wood who raped her, well, the state could point to trial testimony from Kelling's sister, who said Judith identified David Wood as the rapist right after it happened. Greg could go after the sister's credibility, but the question remains, why would Judith Kelling make a grand revelation to Ramona, a teenager she just met?
<v Maurice Chammah>拉莫娜的整个故事显然是疯狂的。当我们结束Whataburger的访谈时，我不清楚格雷格打算如何利用这一切，他是否打算试图证实这些细节。我不确定这真的可能。我可以想象检察官会怎么说。例如，朱迪思·凯林的故事，说强奸她的不是大卫·伍德，好吧，州政府可以指出凯林姐妹在审判中的证词，她说朱迪思在事情发生后立刻指认大卫·伍德为强奸犯。格雷格可以攻击那个姐妹的可信度，但问题仍然存在，为什么朱迪思·凯林要向她刚认识的一个青少年拉莫娜透露一个惊人的秘密？

00:44:57.020 --> 00:45:20.940
<v Maurice Chammah>There's also a question about Ramona's motivations. I found an old news story that says she was banned from the El Paso County Jail. She had allegedly graffitied "I love David Wood" on the wall of a visiting booth. Ramona denies it and says people were just out to tarnish her reputation because she was questioning the police department's version of events. Still, not a great thing to have out there on the internet.
<v Maurice Chammah>关于拉莫娜的动机也有问题。我找到一篇旧新闻报道，说她被禁止进入埃尔帕索县监狱。据称她在探视区的墙上涂鸦了“我爱大卫·伍德”。拉莫娜否认了这一点，说人们只是想抹黑她的名声，因为她质疑警察局对事件的描述。不过，在互联网上出现这些事总归不太好。

00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:53.380
<v Maurice Chammah>But what's most useful to Greg, I think, are all those moments when the cops appeared to have tunnel vision. For example, when Ramona says they tried to get her to falsely accuse David Wood or when she says they ignored the Plyler story, especially if the state didn't tell Wood's lawyers about this stuff before his trial. I wouldn't call these bombshells, but I would call them good evidence. And you could argue that Greg only found it all because the execution date forced him to knock on every door one more time.
<v Maurice Chammah>但我认为对格雷格最有用的，是警察似乎有隧道视野的所有那些时刻。例如，当拉莫娜说他们试图让她虚假指控大卫·伍德时，或者当她说他们忽视了普莱勒的故事时，如果州政府在审判前没有把这些东西告诉伍德的律师，那就更是如此了。我不会把这些称为重磅炸弹，但我会把它们称为好的证据。而且你可以说，格雷格找到这一切只是因为执行日期迫使他再次敲开了每一扇门。

00:45:53.380 --> 00:46:20.140
<v Maurice Chammah>I ended up calling Mike Plyler a few weeks later. He confirmed that he did live in El Paso during the time in question. His tone got a little sharp when I asked if he went by the nickname Skeeter. He interrupted me and said, quote, "That's false." When asked about raping anyone or being the Desert Killer, he said, quote, "I haven't done nothing. I don't have nothing to hide." After we hung up, I sent him Ramona's claims. He ghosted me.
<v Maurice Chammah>几周后我给迈克·普莱勒打了电话。他证实他在相关时期确实住在埃尔帕索。当我问他是否用过“斯基特”这个绰号时，他的语气有点尖锐。他打断我说：“那是假的。”当被问及是否强奸过任何人或是否是“沙漠杀手”时，他说：“我没做什么。我没什么可隐瞒的。”在我们挂断电话后，我给他发了拉莫娜的主张。他不理我了。

00:46:20.140 --> 00:46:53.620
<v Maurice Chammah>I do feel for him. This whole episode made me see just how easy it is for anyone to be accused of pretty egregious stuff in a legal filing, in this case, rape and being a suspect in a serial murder case, all because some stranger mentions your name in a Whataburger. We felt we had to use Plyler's name, given how much it showed up in the lawyer's legal filing, but we also felt like this man deserves every possible opportunity to respond. So after I sent a ton of follow-ups that went unanswered, Alvin and I decided we just had to try in person.
<v Maurice Chammah>我确实同情他。整个节目让我看到，仅仅因为某个陌生人在Whataburger提到你的名字，任何人就很容易在法律文件中被指控犯有相当严重的罪行，在这种情况下是强奸和作为连环谋杀案的嫌疑人。考虑到他的名字在律师的法律文件中出现得如此频繁，我们觉得必须使用普莱勒的名字，但也觉得这个人应该得到每一个可能的回应机会。所以在发了一堆无人回复的跟进信息后，阿尔文和我觉得我们必须当面尝试。

00:46:53.620 --> 00:47:31.140
<v Maurice Chammah>Plyler lives in a mid-sized city in the south. When we got to his house, there were a couple of warning signs about a pitbull, and then we heard barking. I did see a dog through the window-- pretty cute, actually. Plyler opened up just to tell us, quote, "I'm not interested." I asked if I could give him a folder with Ramona's claims. He suggested I throw it in the garbage. I leaned over to put it on the doormat, and by the time I stood back up, the door was closed. The whole thing lasted maybe 10 seconds. I haven't been able to reach him since.
<v Maurice Chammah>普莱勒住在南部的一个中型城市。当我们到达他家时，有几个关于斗牛犬的警告标志，然后我们听到了狗叫声。我确实透过窗户看到了一只狗——实际上相当可爱。普莱勒打开门只是为了告诉我们：“我不感兴趣。”我问是否可以给他一个文件夹，里面装着拉莫娜的主张。他建议我把它扔进垃圾桶。我弯下腰把它放在门垫上，当我站起来时，门已经关上了。整个过程持续了大约10秒。从那以后我再也联系不上他了。

