00:48:16.220 --> 00:48:34.460
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's show, There's Something About Hail Mary. As I said earlier, we're spending this entire hour in the last two minutes of the fourth quarter with people who are behind and desperate, trying any damn thing they can think of. We've arrived at Act Two of our program, Act Two, Bottle Episode.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目主题是“关于万福玛丽的一些事”。正如我之前所说，我们要把整个小时都花在第四节的最后两分钟里，与那些落后、绝望，尝试任何能想到的该死办法的人们在一起。我们进入节目的第二幕，瓶中插曲。

00:48:34.460 --> 00:48:53.420
<v Ira Glass>What is the plural of Hail Mary? Is it Hail Marys? It seems like it should be the "hail" part that gets to be the part that's plural because that's the action, right? That's the thing that's happening. But just Hails Mary just sounds weird. I ask this because Miki Meek has this story where you need the plural.
<v Ira Glass>万福玛丽（Hail Mary）的复数是什么？是Hail Marys吗？似乎应该是“Hail”这个词变成复数，因为那是动作，对吧？那是正在发生的事情。但Hails Mary听起来很奇怪。我问这个是因为米基·米克（Miki Meek）有这个故事，你需要用到复数。

00:48:53.420 --> 00:49:13.060
<v Miki Meek>Aisha Wallace Palomares came across this thing sort of by accident. She's an immigration reporter for a website called LA Taco. And she heard about a lot of people gathering every week outside a detention center called Otay Mesa in San Diego. It's in a pretty desolate spot, surrounded by industrial parks and mountains with off-roading trails.
<v Miki Meek>阿伊莎·华莱士·帕洛马雷斯（Aisha Wallace Palomares）偶然发现了这件事。她是名为“LA Taco”网站的移民记者。她听说每周都有很多人聚集在圣地亚哥一个叫奥泰梅萨（Otay Mesa）的拘留中心外。那是一个相当荒凉的地方，被工业园区和带有越野小径的山脉所包围。

00:49:13.060 --> 00:49:42.660
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>I think, actually, when I first walked up, I think Kiss was playing. [CHUCKLES] A Kiss song was playing. And I just remember very cozy, very friendly, very positive. Even though this was across-- you're looking at this detention center that has beige walls and two layers of fence with barbed wire. And it's just a very stark contrast.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>我想实际上，我第一次走过去时，我想正在放KISS乐队的歌。[笑声] 在放一首KISS的歌。我只记得非常舒适、非常友好、非常积极。尽管这是在对面——你正在看着这个有着米色墙壁和两层带刺铁丝网围栏的拘留中心。这是一种非常鲜明的对比。

00:49:42.660 --> 00:50:05.860
<v Miki Meek>It was a whole group of people, about 150 who brought their dogs and kids. They wanted the people inside to know they were there, which is kind of a puzzle because of the fences, hence the music, which they played pretty loud. They also flew kites, and the kids blew bubbles, hoping to get some of them over the detention center's walls. Aisha watched a pastor walk up to a microphone and speaker that was set up.
<v Miki Meek>那是一大群人，大约150人，带着他们的狗和孩子。他们想让里面的人知道他们在那里，这有点像个谜，因为有围栏，所以他们把音乐放得很大声。他们还放了风筝，孩子们吹泡泡，希望能让其中一些飘过拘留中心的墙壁。阿伊莎看着一位牧师走近设置好的麦克风和扬声器。

00:50:05.860 --> 00:50:13.860
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>And he turned around and faced the detention center, and he said, all right, everyone, I want you guys to yell, you are not alone!
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>他转过身面对拘留中心，说：“好的，大家，我要你们大声喊，你们不孤单！”

00:50:13.860 --> 00:50:18.320
<v Pastor>You are not alone. Let's say it to the hostages behind us.
<v Pastor>你们不孤单。让我们对我们身后的人质说。

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:20.360
<v Crowd>You are not alone!
<v Crowd>你们不孤单！

00:50:20.360 --> 00:50:21.700
<v Pastor>So two more times.
<v Pastor>再来两次。

00:50:21.700 --> 00:50:24.820
<v Crowd>You are not alone!
<v Crowd>你们不孤单！

00:50:24.820 --> 00:50:39.500
<v Miki Meek>There are more than 1,300 detainees inside Otay Mesa. And a lot of them don't have a good, reliable way to communicate with the outside world. In theory, they should be able to make phone calls. There are these tablets they can use to text or do video chats.
<v Miki Meek>奥泰梅萨里有超过1300名被拘留者。他们很多人没有与外部世界沟通的好、可靠的方式。理论上，他们应该能打电话。他们有这些可以用作发短信或视频聊天的平板电脑。

00:50:39.500 --> 00:51:00.540
<v Miki Meek>But the reality is that they have to pay to do any of that. And a lot of people land in detention without any money. And sometimes their families have no idea where they've been taken. They can try to look it up online in ICE's Detainee Locator, but that's not always accurate or up-to-date. And if you're wondering, don't people have a right to a free phone call? The answer is no.
<v Miki Meek>但现实是，他们得付钱才能做任何这些事。很多人在没有任何钱的情况下被拘留。有时他们的家人根本不知道他们被带到了哪里。他们可以尝试在移民局的被拘留者定位系统中在线查找，但这并不总是准确或最新的。如果你在想，人们难道没有免费打电话的权利吗？答案是没有。

00:51:00.540 --> 00:51:06.340
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>And then I just start to notice that they're calling over the fence.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>然后我开始注意到他们正在隔着围栏呼叫。

00:51:06.340 --> 00:51:09.180
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>[SHOUTING]
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>[呼喊声]

00:51:09.180 --> 00:51:12.460
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>The organizers ask for A-numbers.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>组织者要求提供A号码。

00:51:12.460 --> 00:51:14.460
<v Miki Meek>Can you just explain what's an A-number?
<v Miki Meek>你能解释一下什么是A号码吗？

00:51:14.460 --> 00:51:30.180
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>Yeah, it's called an alien number. It's basically a number that is designated to someone who is a non-citizen. And A-numbers are really important for family members to locate their loved ones when they've been detained.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>是的，它被称为外籍人士号码。它基本上是指定给非公民身份的人的号码。对于家人在他们被拘留时定位亲人来说，A号码非常重要。

00:51:30.180 --> 00:51:43.500
<v Miki Meek>If they can get someone's A-number, organizers can put money directly on their books. The organizers then started shushing people. Everyone went silent, which Aisha thought was kind of strange until--
<v Miki Meek>如果他们能得到某人的A号码，组织者可以直接把钱打到他们的账上。组织者开始让人们安静下来。所有人都沉默了，阿伊莎觉得这有点奇怪，直到——

00:51:43.500 --> 00:51:47.300
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>I heard people yell from inside the detention center.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>我听到人们从拘留中心里面大喊大叫。

00:51:47.300 --> 00:51:49.740
<v Woman>[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
<v Woman>[非英语讲话]

00:51:49.740 --> 00:51:51.700
<v Miki Meek>Wow.
<v Miki Meek>哇。

00:51:51.700 --> 00:52:07.600
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>Someone was saying, Russia. So at first, the organizers thought that they were requesting someone who speaks Russian. But then they were realizing that the person inside was telling them that the A-number they were sharing was Russian.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>有人在喊，俄罗斯。起初，组织者以为他们是在要求找一个会说俄语的人。但随后他们意识到里面的人是在告诉他们，他们分享的A号码是俄罗斯人的。

00:52:07.600 --> 00:52:11.300
<v Organizer>OK, Russia. [SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Organizer>好的，俄罗斯。[说西班牙语]

00:52:11.300 --> 00:52:34.210
<v Miki Meek>They had translators on hand for at least 13 languages, including Arabic, Russian, Swahili, and Tagalog. A couple people stood by with notebooks and wrote A-numbers into them. They then immediately tried sending money through these apps, up to $20 for phone calls and text messages and another $45 for commissary. That was the goal-- get as many A-numbers as possible.
<v Miki Meek>他们手头有至少13种语言的翻译，包括阿拉伯语、俄语、斯瓦希里语和他加禄语。几个人拿着笔记本站在旁边，把A号码写进去。然后他们立即尝试通过这些应用程序汇款，最高20美元用于打电话和发短信，另外45美元用于购买杂货。这就是目标——尽可能多地获取A号码。

00:52:34.210 --> 00:52:37.980
<v Miki Meek>[SHOUTING]
<v Miki Meek>[呼喊声]

00:52:37.980 --> 00:52:41.860
<v Miki Meek>It was hard to hear, even when everyone was quiet.
<v Miki Meek>即使在每个人都安静时也很难听清。

00:52:41.860 --> 00:52:45.500
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>This is not like the easiest way to communicate, right?
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>这不是最简单的交流方式，对吧？

00:52:45.500 --> 00:52:49.420
<v Miki Meek>Like, have you heard of this kind of thing happening at other detention centers before?
<v Miki Meek>比如，你以前听说过这种事情发生在其他拘留中心吗？

00:52:49.420 --> 00:52:56.140
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>No, I have not. This is the first time that I had heard anything like this before.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>没有，我没有。这是我第一次听说这样的事。

00:52:56.140 --> 00:53:09.980
<v Miki Meek>Detainees were yelling from different outdoor yards, little patches of concrete with a single basketball hoop. So at some point, someone started bringing a listening device with a parabolic dish. And it's not the only way they communicated.
<v Miki Meek>被拘留者从不同的室外操场大喊大叫，那些是一小块水泥地，带一个篮球架。所以有人开始带着带抛物面天线的监听设备。而且这并不是他们交流的唯一方式。

00:53:09.980 --> 00:53:27.020
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>I guess, there's a drain in one of the yards, or I'm not sure if it's in every yard. And so some of the detainees started kneeling and yelling through the hole.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>我想，其中一个操场有个排水管，或者我不确定每个操场是不是都有。所以一些被拘留者开始跪着对着洞口喊叫。

00:53:27.020 --> 00:53:53.950
<v Miki Meek>The organizers, who call themselves the Otay Mesa Detention Collective, told Aisha that the first time they realized they could communicate through bullhorns and shouting was shortly after they started gathering outside the detention center last fall. They were giving out tamales and bags of fruit to families coming for visiting hours, when they heard a woman yelling from inside the detention center. She was yelling on behalf of another woman who was inside and far from her family. Here's a recording of that.
<v Miki Meek>自称为奥泰梅萨拘留集体的组织者告诉阿伊莎，他们第一次意识到可以通过扩音器和喊叫进行交流，是在去年秋天开始聚集在拘留中心外后不久。他们给来探视时间的家庭分发墨西哥粽和水果袋时，听到一个女人从拘留中心里面大喊。她在为一个在里面、离家人很远的另一个女人呼喊。这里有一段录音。

00:53:53.950 --> 00:53:56.230
<v Woman>She's from Oxnard, 19 years old, and she's here!
<v Woman>她来自奥克斯纳德，19岁，她就在这儿！

00:53:56.230 --> 00:54:03.110
<v Organizer>She's only 19 years old? What's her name? She's from Oxnard, California. She wants her family to know she's here. What's her name?
<v Organizer>她只有19岁？她叫什么名字？她来自加利福尼亚州的奥克斯纳德。她想让她家人知道她在这儿。她叫什么名字？

00:54:03.110 --> 00:54:19.790
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>Her name was Yulissa, and her family did not know where she was. And so they were asking if the organizers could connect with the family or just get some help so that her family could know that she was in Otay Mesa.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>她的名字叫尤丽莎，她的家人不知道她在哪儿。所以他们问组织者能否联系家人，或者只是提供帮助，以便她的家人知道她在奥泰梅萨。

00:54:19.790 --> 00:54:27.430
<v Woman>I just want to get deported because I want to get out of here! [CRYING]
<v Woman>我只是想被驱逐出境，因为我想离开这儿！[哭泣]

00:54:27.430 --> 00:54:52.310
<v Miki Meek>One of the organizers posted video of that exchange on Instagram, and it went viral. Someone responded in a comment, "She's my in-law," and then set up a GoFundMe that said Yulissa's family had been looking for her for a week. Until then, all they knew was that she never showed up to pick up her one-year-old daughter from daycare.
<v Miki Meek>其中一名组织者在Instagram上发布了那段交流的视频，它火了。有人在评论中回应：“她是我的姻亲，”然后建立了一个GoFundMe页面，说尤丽莎的家人已经找了她一个星期。在此之前，他们所知道的只是她没有去日托中心接她一岁的女儿。

00:54:52.310 --> 00:55:05.470
<v Miki Meek>When Aisha first saw that video, she thought it was something that had happened once. She didn't realize it was happening all the time until she was there. In the middle of all these A-numbers being shouted out, another thing happened.
<v Miki Meek>当阿伊莎第一次看到那段视频时，她以为那是发生过一次的事情。直到她身临其境，才意识到这一直在发生。在所有被喊出的A号码中，另一件事发生了。

00:55:05.470 --> 00:55:19.510
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>I was just observing, and I saw people scramble, the organizer scramble and say, there's a bottle, there's a bottle! And then I see a CoreCivic truck start to drive down.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>我只是在观察，看到人们争先恐后，组织者争先恐后地说，有个瓶子，有个瓶子！然后我看到一辆CoreCivic卡车开始开过去。

00:55:19.510 --> 00:55:22.630
<v Miki Meek>CoreCivic is the private company that runs this detention center.
<v Miki Meek>CoreCivic是经营这个拘留中心的私营公司。

00:55:22.630 --> 00:55:36.950
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>It was a game of who was going to get there first, the CoreCivic security truck or the vigil attendee. And one of the vigil attendees ended up grabbing the bottle and bringing it back. And then it was like a bottle. Like, what?
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>这是一场谁先到达那里的游戏，是CoreCivic保安卡车还是守夜参加者。其中一名守夜参加者最终抓住了瓶子并把它带了回来。那就像一个瓶子。比如，什么？

00:55:36.950 --> 00:55:37.730
<v Miki Meek>What kind of bottle was it?
<v Miki Meek>那是什么样的瓶子？

00:55:37.730 --> 00:56:17.070
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>It was a lotion bottle. You know, like, a travel-sized lotion bottle? And I was just, in that moment, like, someone threw a bottle from inside the detention facility. It's a pretty wide distance to throw something over. They have to throw it from inside of this pod, yard, over the cement wall, and then it has to go across-- I mean, I'm not sure how tall this fence is, maybe 14 to 20 feet tall, and it has barbed wire on it. And that's one fence. Then there's a little gap, and there's another fence. And it's also tall and barbed wire.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>那是一个乳液瓶。你知道，那种旅行装的乳液瓶吗？就在那一刻，我心想，有人从拘留设施里面扔了一个瓶子。扔过一个东西是很远的距离。他们得从这个吊舱、操场里面扔出来，越过水泥墙，然后它得越过——我的意思是，我不确定这个围栏有多高，也许14到20英尺高，上面有带刺的铁丝网。那是一个围栏。然后有一个小缺口，有另一个围栏。它也很高，也有带刺铁丝网。

00:56:17.070 --> 00:56:18.410
<v Miki Meek>That is really wild.
<v Miki Meek>这真的很疯狂。

00:56:18.410 --> 00:56:22.230
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>Yeah, and I was just-- I had chills. I was like, that's insane.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>是的，我当时——我起了鸡皮疙瘩。我心想，这太疯狂了。

00:56:22.230 --> 00:56:29.870
<v Miki Meek>I measured it on Google Maps. The shortest distance they would have had to throw the lotion bottle is about 90 feet.
<v Miki Meek>我在谷歌地图上测量了。他们必须扔掉那个乳液瓶的最短距离大约是90英尺。

00:56:29.870 --> 00:56:40.950
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>I noticed that everyone kind of got excited. And then I think-- I don't know if it was Bleu or Holly walks over to me and says, there was a note attached to the bottle. And I was like, what?
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>我注意到每个人都感到有点兴奋。然后我想——我不确定是Bleu还是Holly走过来对我说，瓶子上附着一张纸条。我当时想，什么？

00:56:40.950 --> 00:56:42.970
<v Miki Meek>Is it attached to it, or is it inside of it?
<v Miki Meek>它是附在上面的，还是在里面的？

00:56:42.970 --> 00:56:54.950
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>It's attached to it. I think they used the sticker on the bottle to use that adhesive to somehow attach the note to that. And then that's what it was wrapped on.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>它是附在上面的。我想他们用了瓶子上的贴纸来利用粘合剂以某种方式把纸条附在那个上面。然后那就是它被包裹的东西。

00:56:54.950 --> 00:56:56.870
<v Miki Meek>So Bleu brings it over to you, and--
<v Miki Meek>所以Bleu把它带过来给你，然后——

00:56:56.870 --> 00:57:05.830
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>She says, do you want to hold it? My stomach kind of dropped, and I said, yes. And I'm just holding this note in my hands, and I'm reading it.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>她说，你想拿着吗？我的胃有点下沉，我说，是的。我就拿着这张纸条，我正在读它。

00:57:05.830 --> 00:57:08.470
<v Miki Meek>I was wondering, can you read that message for me?
<v Miki Meek>我在想，你能为我读一下那条信息吗？

00:57:08.470 --> 00:57:49.510
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>Yeah, I can. "Good afternoon. My wife and I have been at OMDC since April 15, 2025. It's cold here all the time, and the food is poor. For 290 days, we haven't eaten a single piece of fruit, banana, apple, orange, or anything fresh. We are all in one big room with no doors or windows. We can't see any grass or trees. We are all constantly sick. There is no internet. My lawyer was not given my phone calls. Many people here have been sitting for 12, 14, 16, 18 months without a final court decision."
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>是的，我可以。“下午好。我和我妻子自2025年4月15日起就在OMDC了。这里一直很冷，食物很差。290天来，我们没吃过一块水果、香蕉、苹果、橙子或任何新鲜东西。我们都挤在一个没有门窗的大房间里。我们看不到任何草地或树木。我们都一直生病。没有互联网。我的律师没有收到我的电话。这里很多人在没有最终法庭裁决的情况下已经坐了12、14、16、18个月了。”

00:57:49.510 --> 00:57:57.830
<v Miki Meek>Aisha asked the main organizer, a preschool teacher with bright blue hair named Jeane Wong, is this the first time someone had thrown a note over to them?
<v Miki Meek>阿伊莎问主要组织者，一位留着亮蓝色头发的学前班老师珍·黄，这是有人第一次扔纸条给他们吗？

00:57:57.830 --> 00:58:19.590
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>And then Jeane tells me that this is not the first note, that there are several notes that they've received, but this is the longest note that they've had. Most of them have had A-numbers, but she says that this person from inside was so desperate to have their story told that they had actually thrown another bottle with the same exact note earlier that day.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>然后珍告诉我，这不是第一张纸条，他们收到过好几张，但这是他们收到的最长的纸条。大多数都有A号码，但她说这个里面的人如此绝望想要讲出他们的故事，以至于他们当天早些时候扔了另一个带有完全相同纸条的瓶子。

00:58:19.590 --> 00:58:21.630
<v Miki Meek>What kind of other objects have come over?
<v Miki Meek>还有什么其他东西被扔过来了？

00:58:21.630 --> 00:58:27.230
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>Lotion bottles, deodorant bottles, and I think even a AA battery.
<v Aisha Wallace-Palomares>乳液瓶、除臭剂瓶，我想甚至还有一节AA电池。

00:58:27.230 --> 00:58:51.950
<v Miki Meek>Most of what they got that day were A-numbers. Aisha says about 50, mostly from people shouting and throwing bottles over with multiple numbers scrawled on them. I reached out to CoreCivic about the conditions described in the letter that Aisha read. They denied all the allegations, called them flatly false and inconsistent with their standards.
<v Miki Meek>那天他们收到的大多是A号码。阿伊莎说大约50个，大多来自人们大喊大叫，并扔过来带有乱涂乱画的多个号码的瓶子。我联系了CoreCivic关于阿伊莎读到的信件中描述的情况。他们否认了所有指控，称它们完全是虚假的，与他们的标准不符。

00:58:51.950 --> 00:59:19.910
<v Miki Meek>There have been reports for decades about inhumane living conditions and poor medical care at Otay Mesa and other ICE detention facilities. But these problems have gotten a lot worse since President Trump returned to office, because the number of people in detention has increased so dramatically, things like overcrowding, not getting enough food, or getting food that's spoiled, also, physical and verbal abuse from guards. One of the biggest issues right now-- detainees needing medical treatment and not getting it.
<v Miki Meek>几十年来，一直有关于奥泰梅萨和其他移民局拘留中心存在不人道生活条件和医疗保健差的报道。但自从特朗普总统重返办公室以来，这些问题变得更糟了，因为拘留人数增加得如此巨大，像过度拥挤、食物不够，或得到变质的食物，还有来自警卫的身体和言语虐待。现在最大的问题之一——被拘留者需要医疗护理却得不到。

00:59:19.910 --> 00:59:35.230
<v Miki Meek>Over the past year, the death rate in ICE custody has more than doubled compared to recent years, more than 50 people since President Trump came back to office. One of them was a Haitian man named Emmanuel Damas. He went into septic shock from a toothache.
<v Miki Meek>在过去的一年里，与近年来相比，移民局拘留所的死亡率翻了一倍多，特朗普总统回来后已经有超过50人死亡。其中一人是名叫伊曼纽尔·达马斯的海地男子。他因牙疼引发感染性休克而死亡。

00:59:35.230 --> 01:00:06.430
<v Miki Meek>It's worth noting that most people in ICE detention facilities do not have a criminal record. Crossing the border illegally or overstaying a visa is not a criminal offense. It's a civil violation. And immigration detention is not supposed to be punitive like a prison. Many immigration lawyers believe the administration is trying to wear people down by holding them for long periods in these harsh conditions, so they'll give up on their cases and agree to deportation.
<v Miki Meek>值得注意的是，移民局拘留中心的大多数人都没有犯罪记录。非法越境或签证逾期不是刑事犯罪。这是一种民事违规。移民拘留不应该像监狱那样具有惩罚性。许多移民律师认为，政府试图通过将人们长期关押在这些恶劣环境中来消磨他们的意志，这样他们就会放弃案件并同意被驱逐出境。

01:00:06.430 --> 01:00:17.850
<v Miki Meek>While Aisha was there, some of the detainees who'd gotten money put into their accounts sent text messages to the organizers. They requested music they wanted organizers to blast. Others wanted their texts read out loud.
<v Miki Meek>当阿伊莎在那里时，一些钱被存入账户的被拘留者给组织者发送了短信。他们要求组织者播放他们想要的音乐。其他人想要他们的短信被大声朗读出来。

01:00:17.850 --> 01:00:22.510
<v Organizer 1>We just got a text from someone inside, and so she's going to read it for everybody here, OK?
<v Organizer 1>我们刚从里面收到一条短信，所以她要读给这里的每个人听，好吗？

01:00:22.510 --> 01:00:30.830
<v Organizer 2>I put it into Google Translate, and it said, are you guys outside? We can hear you. We can hear you. We can hear you. We love you. Thank you.
<v Organizer 2>我把它放进谷歌翻译，上面说，你们在外面吗？我们能听到你们。我们能听到你们。我们能听到你们。我们爱你们。谢谢。

01:00:30.830 --> 01:00:32.750
<v Organizer 2>[CHEERING, APPLAUSE]
<v Organizer 2>[欢呼，掌声]

01:00:32.750 --> 01:00:56.390
<v Miki Meek>At the end of the day, the organizers asked Aisha, please don't publish anything about how we're communicating with detainees. They worried it would all get shut down. But then they texted the people inside, including the person who threw over the lotion bottle, and asked what they thought. The answer? Publish it. The detainees wanted the story out. They wanted people to know what was happening.
<v Miki Meek>在一天结束时，组织者问阿伊莎，请不要发表任何关于我们如何与被拘留者沟通的内容。他们担心这一切会被关闭。但随后他们给里面的人发了短信，包括扔出乳液瓶的人，问他们怎么想。答案是什么？发表。被拘留者想要这个故事流传出去。他们想要人们知道发生了什么。

01:00:56.390 --> 01:01:32.950
<v Miki Meek>Aisha's story ran, and then, as predicted, there was retaliation, according to detainees. They said that CoreCivic turned down temperatures, took away showers, limited tablet access and commissary time, and shut down the yard on Sunday afternoons when the organizers are outside, which means no more messages on bottles thrown over fences. But it doesn't matter. The organizers now have enough A-numbers that they're communicating with hundreds of detainees by text.
<v Miki Meek>阿伊莎的故事发表了，然后正如预期的那样，据被拘留者称，发生了报复。他们说CoreCivic降低了设施内的温度，剥夺了洗澡机会，限制了平板电脑使用和购买杂货的时间，并关闭了周日下午组织者在外面时的操场，这意味着不再有扔过围栏的纸条瓶子了。但这没关系。组织者现在有足够的A号码，他们正在通过短信与数百名被拘留者沟通。

01:01:32.950 --> 01:01:56.030
<v Ira Glass>Miki Meek is a producer on our show. When we reached out to CoreCivic, they denied that temperatures inside the facility were kept deliberately uncomfortable, and they said they have a zero-tolerance policy for retaliation against detainees. ICE did not respond to our request for comment. You can find more of Aisha Wallace Palomares's reporting at the website, LA Taco.
